{
  "name": "vendor-schema",
  "integration": "forescout-eyeinspect",
  "description": "REGRESSION LOCK on the real Host record property names, taken from the eyeInspect API Guide's Record Type Definitions rather than from the sort_field enum. The two vocabularies are NOT the same list: sorting on 'name' returns 'main_name', and the same holds for role and vendor_model. Reading role, vendor_model and complex_cves instead -- none of which are response keys -- leaves deviceType unset, names every firmware Software row 'Firmware', and empties host CVE findings even with full=true. The open_ports transport key is l4_protocol, not l4_proto, so getting that wrong imports every UDP service as tcp. CVEs arrive as a HostCVEInfo array: the suppressed entry is an operator dismissal and must not become a finding, and the ICS-CERT-only entry has an empty cve_id. Exactly one CVE finding must survive.\n\nIt also covers the Full-only properties. 'software' is a SoftwareInfo[] of {install_date, version, name, vendor} -- a direct match for runZero's Software, whose product field is 'product' and whose id is mandatory -- and 'patches' is a PatchesInfo[] of {hot_fix_id, installed_by, install_date, service_pack}. Three application rows, one patch row and the synthetic firmware row must all appear, with vendor and version carried through. OpenPortsInfo's third member 'app' ('The service/application running on the port. Example: HTTP') must reach the Service as its protocol name.\n\nThe value domains are the guide's: criticality is an integer in [0-5] (not the word 'high'), purdue_level is the string enum LEVEL0..LEVEL5/LEVEL35/UNDEFINED (not '1'), and security_risk and operational_risk are floats. host_mac_address is a HostMacAddress[] of objects, not a string[], and the guide spells the sensor id list 'sensors_ids'. alert_count is NOT a Host record field and is absent from the record: it exists only in the sort_field enum.",
